{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-group",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "social group"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "A collection of two or more individuals who interact with one another, share a sense of identity or belonging, and are connected through shared purposes, goals, norms, values, or expectations."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "Individuals may belong to multiple social groups simultaneously, and these groups may vary in duration and structure. Three types of social groups can be defined: 1) primary groups, which are small, intimate, emotionally close groups such as families or close friends; 2) secondary groups, which are larger, more impersonal, goal‑oriented groups such as work teams or organisations, and 3) reference groups, which are groups that individuals use as standards for self‑evaluation. Social groups differ from aggregates, which consist of people gathered in the same place without meaningful interaction, and from categories, which include people who share a characteristic but lack interaction or a shared identity."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "3.16"
  ],
